WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.413
Compactness says every open cover has a finite subcover

00:00:04.413 --> 00:00:11.992
In Euclidean spaces, the Heine Borel theorem identifies compact sets as exactly the closed and bounded ones

00:00:11.992 --> 00:00:18.233
The closed interval is our basic example, not the definition for every topological space.

00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:25.947
A continuous real-valued function on a nonempty compact set attains its maximum and minimum

00:00:25.947 --> 00:00:30.528
On this interval, the identity reaches its maximum at one

00:00:30.528 --> 00:00:35.567
Removing that endpoint leaves values approaching one without ever attaining it.

00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:44.244
Connectedness is a different property: the space cannot be separated into two nonempty relatively open pieces

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.089
Real intervals are connected

00:00:46.089 --> 00:00:53.467
Consequently, a continuous image of an interval cannot skip intermediate values between two of its outputs.

00:00:54.767 --> 00:01:01.448
Two separated closed intervals still form a closed bounded set, hence a compact one

00:01:01.448 --> 00:01:06.220
They are disconnected because the gap separates the two components

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.515
Compactness controls covers and limiting behavior; connectedness controls separation

00:01:10.515 --> 00:01:14.333
One property does not automatically supply the other.
